    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Complete the seven loading doses of cyanocobalamin 1,000 micrograms every other day, then 1,000 micrograms weekly by self-injection until neurological improvement levels off, then monthly for life. [S]</item><item>High-dose oral cyanocobalamin (at least 1 mg a day) only if he prefers it once recovered; intramuscular maintenance is the default. [S]</item><item>No transfusion while he is stable at rest; repeat potassium at the next visit. [S]</item><item>Blood count at about five and eight weeks; methylmalonic acid, homocysteine and iron studies at eight weeks; treat iron deficiency if it appears. [S]</item><item>Upper endoscopy with Sydney-protocol biopsies, with a screening colonoscopy at the same session; set the surveillance interval from the pathology by shared decision. [S]</item><item>TSH yearly; HbA1c with routine screening. [S]</item><item>Neurological examination at each visit, physical therapy for balance, night lights and a stick on rough ground. [S]</item><item>Daily foot checks, fitted footwear for the amputated forefeet, and podiatry. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Lindgren-Okafor, who saw him the same day. The diagnosis, the reason for lifelong injections, and the endoscopy were explained to Admiral Peary, who asked how long recovery would take and whether this was the illness that had no cure in the past; he was told that it is now fully treatable, though nerve recovery can be incomplete. He learned to self-inject today. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Dr. Lindgren-Okafor continues care at five, eight and fifteen weeks; hematology will see him again if the count does not normalize, iron deficiency does not respond, or a neuroendocrine tumor is found. [S]</item></list></text>

